    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Cairo grew from a chain of settlements, Babylon Fortress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, al-Askar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> built al-Qahira in 969 as a palace city and the later rulers opened it to the public.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> the city reached close to half a million people by 1340, then lost many in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> and in the shift of the spice trade to the Cape route.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> Cairo became a provincial capital, and under Muhammad Ali and Isma'il it was rebuilt in part on European lines, becoming the capital of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1168 the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar set fire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> king Amalric could not take it. The fire did not destroy the city, but it began its decline, and Cairo took over its trade.</p></aside>
    </section>

