    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">641 or 642</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> beside Babylon Fortress after the Muslim conquest</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">969</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> al-Saqili founds the city that becomes Cairo</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1176</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> begins the Citadel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1250</span><span class="rf-text">Power passes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1348</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> strikes the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1517</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Cairo becomes a provincial capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1798</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> arrives in Cairo</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1952</span><span class="rf-text">The Cairo Fire destroys nearly 700 shops, cinemas, casinos and hotels in the Downtown</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocletian.jpg" alt="Diocletian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_Bust_of_Diocletian,_from_Nikomedia,_Izmit,_end_3rd_century_AD_1536.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The land around Cairo lies where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> meets the Delta, roughly the junction of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Memphis, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the Old Kingdom and a major city until the Ptolemaic period, stood a short distance south west of the present city. Heliopolis, an important religious centre, lay in what are now Matariya and Ain Shams in the north east. The Persian invasions of 525 BCE and 343 BCE largely destroyed it, and it was partly abandoned by the late first century BCE.</p><p>The origins of the modern city are generally traced to settlements of the first millennium CE. The trading post of Babylon, first mentioned in 50 BCE, became a fortress built by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> (r. 285 to 305) at the entrance of a canal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> (r. 98 to 117) had created. North of it was a port and outpost called Tendunyas or Umm Dunayn. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, Cyrus, took part in the Second Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 449. The Byzantine-Sassanian War of 602 to 628 caused hardship and probably led much of the urban population to leave for the countryside. The oldest surviving churches, among them Saint Barbara and Saints Sergius and Bacchus, stand inside the fortress walls in what is now Old Cairo, or Coptic Cairo.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Ancient settlements, Fustat and other early Islamic settlements, Foundation and expansion of Cairo under the Fatimids:</p><ul><li>Gawdat Gabra, The History and Religious Heritage of Old Cairo: Its Fortress, Churches, Synagogue, and Mosque (American University in Cairo Press, 2013)</li><li>Janet L. Abu-Lughod, Cairo: 1001 Years of the City Victorious (Princeton University Press, 1971)</li><li>Tarek Swelim, Ibn Tulun: His Lost City and Great Mosque (American University in Cairo Press, 2015)</li><li>André Raymond, Cairo (Harvard University Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh.jpg" alt="Bab al-Futuh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh">Bab al-Futuh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo,_porte_settentrionali,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> reached its greatest size and prosperity as a centre of craftsmanship, international trade and the area's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> port. Sources report multi-storey communal residences there, some of seven floors and housing 200 to 350 people. Cairo itself remained a relatively exclusive royal city for most of the period. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Badr al-Gamali (1073 to 1094) richer families from Fustat were first allowed to move in, and between 1087 and 1092 he rebuilt the walls in stone and raised the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-futuh">Bab al-Futuh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bab-al-nasr">Bab al-Nasr</a> and Bab Zuweila, which still stand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a>, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1176, ended the palace city's exclusiveness and opened it to ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and foreign merchants.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> (1293 to 1341, with interregnums) Cairo reached its apogee in population and wealth. By 1340 it had close to half a million people, making it the largest city west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Rental apartment buildings called rab' became common, and the Mamluks built mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> that pushed the city outward. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was the second-largest city of the empire, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and spread south and west of the Citadel. Between 1882 and 1937 its population more than tripled, from 347,000 to 1.3 million, and its area grew from 10 to 163 square kilometres. Greater Cairo went from 2,986,280 people in 1947 to 16,292,269 in 2006.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Foundation and expansion of Cairo under the Fatimids, Further expansion and decline under the Ayyubids and Mamluks, Economy:</p><ul><li>André Raymond, Cairo (Harvard University Press, 2000)</li><li>M. W. Daly, The Cambridge History of Egypt: Islamic Egypt, 640–1517 (Cambridge University Press, 1998)</li><li>Boaz Shoshan, Popular Culture in Medieval Cairo (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>Nezar AlSayyad, Cairo: Histories of a City (Harvard University Press, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> (639 to 642), led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> became the administrative capital. In 750, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, they laid out al-Askar, a military-style camp, to the northeast as the provincial capital. In 870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, sent to Egypt in 868 with his stepfather Bakbak, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a> as his capital and by 878 was de facto independent ruler of Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a>, was short-lived: in 905 the Abbasids sent a general to reassert direct control and razed al-Qata'i, except for the mosque.</p><p>In 969 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> conquered Egypt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, and al-Mu'izz made the new city his capital in 973. In 1169 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, nephew of the Zengid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, and two years later he seized power from the family of the last Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-'Adid, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> and aligning Egypt with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Abbasids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. From 1250 to 1517 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> held the throne, passing it from one mamluk to another, generally without heredity and often violently. In 1517 the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> made Egypt a province with Cairo as its capital. After the French occupation (1798 to 1801), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a> became viceroy in 1805 and ruled until his death in 1848. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> held the country from 1882, and Egypt became independent in 1922.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Ottoman rule, Modern era:</p><ul><li>André Raymond, Cairo (Harvard University Press, 2000)</li><li>Michael Winter, Egyptian Society Under Ottoman Rule, 1517–1798 (Routledge, 1992)</li><li>David Sims, Understanding Cairo: The Logic of a City Out of Control (American University in Cairo Press, 2012)</li><li>Martin Sicker, The Islamic World in Decline: From the Treaty of Karlowitz to the Disintegration of the Ottoman Empire (Greenwood Publishing Group, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza.jpg" alt="Cairo Geniza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza">Cairo Geniza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bab_voc_fragment_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Kahle, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cairo became a centre of learning under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-azhar">al-Azhar mosque</a>, commissioned during the building of the city, developed into a university, and the library of Cairo held hundreds of thousands of books. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> al-Azhar attained a predominance among Islamic schools that it still holds. Cairo has been the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a> since the 12th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> reported 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> there in 1170, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-geniza">Cairo Geniza</a>, almost 200,000 Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> written from about 870 to 1880, was found in the Ben Ezra Synagogue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, built in 882, and in other places.</p><p>Housing was organised in close-knit neighbourhoods called harat, whose gates could be closed at night or in disturbances. Trade was housed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, the wikalas and khans. Khan el-Khalili dates to 1385, when Amir Jarkas el-Khalili built a large caravanserai, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Ghuri rebuilt in the early 16th century. In the 16th and 17th centuries Cairo carried Yemeni <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and Indian textiles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and its merchants supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, printing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, was set up in Cairo c. 1557; it is known only from two fragments in the Geniza. At the end of the 18th century twenty percent of the population were religious minorities and foreigners from around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Religion, Theatre, Downtown, Old Cairo:</p><ul><li>Caroline Williams, Islamic Monuments in Cairo: The Practical Guide (The American University in Cairo Press, 2008)</li><li>Gawdat Gabra, The History and Religious Heritage of Old Cairo: Its Fortress, Churches, Synagogue, and Mosque (American University in Cairo Press, 2013)</li><li>Caroline Williams, Islamic Monuments in Cairo: The Practical Guide (The American University in Cairo Press, 2018)</li><li>Mohamed Elshahed, Cairo Since 1900: An Architectural Guide (American University in Cairo Press, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mamluk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk.jpg" alt="Mamluk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Mamelukes_with_lances_on_horseback.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Hopfer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1168 the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar set fire to the unfortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> to keep it from Amalric, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The fire did not destroy the city, but it marked the beginning of its decline. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> returned in 1348 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> was ravaging most major cities, and he cited reports of thousands of deaths a day in Cairo. The plague struck the city more than fifty times between 1348 and 1517. In its first and deadliest waves about 200,000 people died, and by the 15th century the population had fallen to between 150,000 and 300,000. Political instability lasted from 1348 to 1412. The city's role in trade was diminished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s sea route round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, between 1497 and 1499, which let spice traders avoid it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> reached Cairo in 1798, when the population was under 300,000, forty percent lower than at the height of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> influence in the mid-14th century. British and Ottoman forces besieged the city, and the French surrendered on 22 June 1801. The 1952 riots known as the Cairo Fire, or Black Saturday, destroyed nearly 700 shops, cinemas, casinos and hotels in the Downtown. An earthquake in 1992 caused 545 deaths, injured 6,512 and left about 50,000 homeless. In the revolution of 2011, which began in Tahrir Square on 25 January, at least 846 people were killed and 6,000 injured; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a> resigned on 11 February.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Further expansion and decline under the Ayyubids and Mamluks:</p><ul><li>André Raymond, Cairo (Harvard University Press, 2000)</li><li>Boaz Shoshan, Popular Culture in Medieval Cairo (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>Doris Behrens-Abouseif, Cairo of the Mamluks: A History of Architecture and its Culture (The American University in Cairo Press, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun.jpg" alt="Mosque of Ibn Tulun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kairo_Ibn_Tulun_Moschee_BW_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berthold Werner, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cairo holds one of the largest concentrations of historical Islamic monuments in the world: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a>, built in 876 to 879 and the oldest mosque that keeps its original form; the Fatimid gates and mosques; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a>-Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Hasan, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> religious monument; and the Mamluk complexes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-muhammad">al-Nasir Muhammad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barquq">Barquq</a>. Its historic centre was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1979. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">The Citadel</a> was the seat of government and residence of its rulers until 1874, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive</a> Isma'il moved to 'Abdin Palace. Isma'il's Downtown, with its squares and boulevards, was inspired by the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-eugene-haussmann">Baron Haussmann</a>. The Nilometer on Roda Island, of the 860s, is the oldest surviving Islamic-era structure in the city.</p><p>The King Fuad edition of the Qur'an, published in Cairo on 10 July 1924, has become the standard for much modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. Since 1952 the city has expanded by new towns in the desert and by informal housing: by 2009 over 63 percent of Greater Cairo's people lived in informal neighbourhoods on 17 percent of its area. In 2015 plans were announced for a new administrative capital east of the city. Cairo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; the city has about nine and a half million people, and the metropolitan area more than 22 million.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Cairo&#8221; cites where it covers Modern era, Downtown, Old Cairo, Islamic Cairo:</p><ul><li>André Raymond, Cairo (Harvard University Press, 2000)</li><li>Caroline Williams, Islamic Monuments in Cairo: The Practical Guide (The American University in Cairo Press, 2008)</li><li>David Sims, Understanding Cairo: The Logic of a City Out of Control (American University in Cairo Press, 2012)</li><li>Gawdat Gabra, The History and Religious Heritage of Old Cairo: Its Fortress, Churches, Synagogue, and Mosque (American University in Cairo Press, 2013)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/ya-mustafa">Ya Mustafa<span>1960</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Cairo grew out of Babylon Fortress and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> founded the city proper in 969. It was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> capital at its height, with close to half a million people by 1340, an Ottoman provincial centre, and then, from the 19th century, the rebuilt capital of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</em></p>

